= Conclusion <chapter:conclusion>

This thesis examined whether #acr("MPC")-guided policy pretraining provides a useful initialization for recurrent chlorination control. This chapter summarizes the findings on online learning, final physical control performance, and preparation cost. We then discuss the contribution and its scope, followed by further work needed to assess the approach beyond the evaluated setting.


== Answer to the Research Question

#acr("MPC")-guided pretraining improved online sample efficiency in the evaluated Hanoi setting. The pretrained approach had a higher mean validation return at every evaluated checkpoint, including before online training. At #num(endpoint-crossing.nominal) nominal interactions, its mean validation return first reached the baseline's return at the one-million-interaction endpoint and remained above this level at all subsequent checkpoints. This comparison shows how much earlier the pretrained approach reached this return level. The crossing was identified retrospectively from validation returns and does not establish equal physical control at that point. The mean return difference became much smaller as the baseline improved. The results therefore support a learning advantage within the investigated budget but do not establish differences in asymptotic performance.

The final test comparison also favored pretraining for contamination treatment. After both approaches completed the common online budget, the policies selected by validation return were evaluated separately on the fixed test cases. The selected pretrained policies had #fixed-pct(100 * (1 - burden-summary.at("pretrained-mean") / burden-summary.at("baseline-mean")), digits: 1) lower mean transported #acr("As(III)") burden and reached persistent clearance #duration-text(clearance-summary.at("difference-mean") * 3600) earlier on average over the paired eligible events. Both differences favored pretraining in all five matched seed pairs. The clearance comparison covers only events in which both policies exceeded the operational threshold, whereas the burden comparison covers all test cases.

These treatment gains were accompanied by higher chlorine use and greater normal-operation chlorine excess. Mean chlorine use increased by #fixed-pct(100 * (use-summary.at("pretrained-mean") / use-summary.at("baseline-mean") - 1), digits: 1), and the reference-normal excess fraction increased by #fixed(100 * excess-summary.at("difference-mean"), digits: 3) percentage points. Together with the lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden and earlier clearance, the higher chlorine use suggests a stronger treatment response under the modeled oxidation reaction. The comparison does not determine how much of the additional chlorine was consumed by oxidation. In the exploratory decomposition, almost all of the excess difference occurred in the first #hour[12] after reference clearance. The direction of the shortfall difference remained uncertain, and neither approach exceeded the #mg-l[5.0] event-time ceiling in the applicable test observations. Under the treatment priority adopted in @section:discussion:trade-offs, the lower burden and earlier clearance favor the pretrained policies despite their higher chlorine use and post-clearance excess. Pretraining therefore improved the treatment balance under this priority but did not improve every control metric.

The online learning advantage did not reduce total computational effort. Across the five seeds, the recorded pipeline stages, shared offline trajectory generation, and control evaluations took a total of #duration-text(pretrained-total-seconds) for the pretrained approach and #duration-text(baseline-total-seconds) for the baseline approach. With fresh preparation, the pretrained approach therefore took about #fixed(pretrained-total-seconds / baseline-total-seconds, digits: 1) times as long, despite a shorter #acr("PPO") stage. It also required offline simulator trajectories in addition to the matched online interactions. Pretraining therefore improved online sample efficiency and final contamination treatment but required additional preparation effort.


== Main Contribution and Scope

The main contribution is an evaluated training pipeline that combines multistep chlorine forecasting, cross-fitted #acr("MPC") teacher generation, behavior cloning, and RecurrentPPO fine-tuning. Scenario-wise cross-fitting reuses the offline trajectories but generates teacher actions for each scenario using a forecaster fitted without that scenario. Behavior cloning trains the recurrent actor to reproduce the teacher actions before it is fine-tuned through reward-based interaction. The forecaster and optimizer are used only during offline preparation. The resulting controller observes only chlorine measurements, its own dosing, and time records.

Both approaches use the same deployed architecture, observations, actuator constraints, reward, and online training conditions. The principal comparison therefore assesses the complete initialization, which includes actor weights, observation normalization, and initial action standard deviation. The comparison with the variant without actor weights supports a contribution from cloned actor weights to validation return, conditional on the other transferred components. The extended-budget baseline shows that additional online training can narrow the return difference, but these additional comparisons do not establish equal physical control. The forecast evaluation provides supporting evidence for the prediction model, while the parameter screening provides practical settings. The final treatment benefit is supported by the replicated policy comparison on separate test cases.

These findings are based on five matched pairs of training runs using the fixed Hanoi scenario partition, the specified contamination process, and chlorine measurements at all 31 demand nodes without added sensor noise. Final control performance refers to the checkpoints selected by validation return after the nominal one-million-interaction budget was completed, following @section:evaluation:policy-selection. Because the offline trajectories and evaluation cases are shared, the repeated runs describe training variability under these conditions. Transfer to other networks, operating periods, contamination processes, or measurement conditions remains untested.

The physical conclusions are limited to the modeled chlorine--arsenite reaction. A lower transported #acr("As(III)") burden indicates improved oxidation in the simulator. It does not demonstrate total arsenic removal or drinking-water safety. The source-injection bounds and reward-based concentration control do not guarantee that nodal chlorine limits will always be satisfied. No event-time ceiling violations were observed in the tested cases, but this does not constitute a hard concentration constraint.


== Future Work

Further comparisons should determine which initialization components are needed to preserve the treatment gains. Separating the effects of actor weights, observation normalization, and initial action standard deviation would clarify their roles in the early learning advantage. Evaluating the cloned policy before fine-tuning and the additional variants with the six physical metrics would also show how their return differences relate to treatment and dosing. More training seeds and independently generated offline datasets would test how strongly the findings depend on the current replication design. Changes to the reward or checkpoint-selection criterion could then be evaluated for their ability to produce a more favorable balance between post-clearance excess, chlorine use, arsenic burden, and earlier clearance.

Reducing preparation cost is another priority, since forecasting accounts for most of the recorded offline runtime. Fewer cross-fitting folds, a single forecaster with separate teacher-query scenarios, or reuse of compatible trajectories, forecasters, and demonstrations could reduce repeated preparation. The total simulator interactions and measured stage runtimes of these alternatives should be evaluated alongside final physical control performance. Such comparisons would show whether the treatment advantage is preserved with a smaller preparation budget and with reused artifacts instead of fresh preparation.

#acr("DAgger") provides a way to investigate the difference between the states in prescribed offline trajectories and those visited by the cloned actor @ross2011dagger. The actor would generate new simulator trajectories, and the teacher would label the query states visited along them. Combining these demonstrations and repeating behavior cloning would train the actor on histories produced by its own earlier decisions. Any improvement would need to be weighed against the additional simulator interactions and #acr("MPC") queries, which the current fixed-dataset design avoids.

The quality of teacher actions should also be assessed when the optimizer searches for sequences with high predicted returns. A teacher inspired by #acr("PETS") could compare candidate actions using probabilistic ensembles and sampled trajectories to account for uncertainty @chua2018pets. This would require validating the uncertainty estimates for the partially observable multistep forecasting task. Comparing candidate forecasts with simulator responses could then test whether accounting for uncertainty reduces reliance on optimistic prediction errors and improves the demonstrations. However, this extension would not by itself guarantee that the optimizer cannot exploit model errors.

Further work could assess whether spatial and temporal representations improve forecasting and policy learning. A graph neural network could encode the network topology before the forecasting model or recurrent actor processes the measurements. Node-specific estimates of injection response or arrival time, conditioned on the time of day and recent dosing, could provide more explicit information about delayed effects. Under the current policy-input restrictions, these estimates would need to be derived from available measurements and dosing histories. They should be evaluated through final control performance as well as prediction accuracy. Improved forecasting accuracy alone does not establish better teacher actions or policy behavior.

Application beyond the evaluated setting requires tests on other topologies, seasonal demand periods, and contamination settings, as well as reduced sensor coverage, noise, missing measurements, and delays. Tests with calibrated transport and reaction models would help assess how much of the treatment balance depends on the synthetic chemistry used here. If concentration limits must be guaranteed, a separate safety mechanism would need to account for delayed network responses and model uncertainty. These evaluations would determine whether the observed gains in online learning and contamination treatment can support chlorination control beyond the simulated setting studied here.
